 ventricular mass at echocardiography in all subjects.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-22-256-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The ambulatory and clinic BP difference in relation to the clinic BP for all subjects. The White-coat hypertension was dominated in subjects with high clinic BP, whereas the masked hypertension was prevalent in those with lower clinic BP.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-22-256-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Baseline characteristics of the study subjects</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-22-256-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>difference between NT and MHT</p><p><sup>&#x02020;</sup>difference between WCHT and SHT</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Echocardiographic and laboratory measurements of the study subjects</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-22-256-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>difference between NT and MHT</p><p><sup>&#x02020;</sup>difference between WCHT and SHT</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Predictors of Masked Hypertension</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-22-256-i003"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T4" position="float"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Predictors of White-coat Hypertension</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-22-256-i004"/></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>